A||N||40||Alex||Seems I Have Everything Except a Car That Runs---Compression?||||13||
Z||000000||Alex||03-12-2001||11:43 AM||||I've just climbed out of the archives for the fourth time in a week. My '87 4 banger with 127,000 miles won't start. Just came out one morning, it fired, died, and is still dead---mostly.<BR>I've replaced plugs, ign wires, Ign Module, one of two coils (I droped one during Module replacement and although it didn't land on anything critical, it tests open between  the two HV terminals). There's a spark from both coils, plenty of gas (perhaps even too much)and it turns over fine.<BR>Often flooded, I've disconnected the injector and tried to  get it to go. Injector or not, it will, after sitting overnight, make a starting sound (flump, flump) then nothing. Hold the pedel to the metal and it will try to start but fail. Will also back fire through carb. No codes.<BR>I checked compression and found a high of 120 and a low of 70. I'm sure it didn't loose compression overnight and it was running well enough before this but could this be the problem? I'm about out of ideas here.<BR>What do you think I should try?||||reg||13
Z||000001||StuGood||03-12-2001||02:58 PM||||Overnight compression loss, and wide variation in compression readings, suggests timing gear trouble, I <B>think</B>.  ||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cplensdorf||03-12-2001||05:59 PM||||I hear the sound of a broken timing chain (gears) ringing in my ears!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex||03-12-2001||08:06 PM||||OK, that's two for timing chain/gears. So I check for TDC on cyl 1 and see if both valves are closed right?||||reg||11
Z||000004||Fiero_Freak||03-12-2001||09:36 PM||||Well you may need to verify that your timing chain is still working properly....or is it a timing gear?  Never messed with the 4 cyl.  Sounds like it could be busted though.  Guys?  Would a simple check be to see if the distributer is rotating...and then check the timing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||862M4inCA||03-12-2001||10:49 PM||||that does sounds suspiciously like a timing gear/chain problem. Possibly jumped a tooth (not really likely on the 4-banger since it would have most likely busted the fiber gear in that case). If the distributor has not been removed since this problem started I would check to make sure I was #1TDC compression and verify it's pointing to the #1 tower. If it's off then I would really suspect the valve timing. If the distributor has been removed then I would check to make sure both valves on #1 comp. are closed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex||03-13-2001||12:41 AM||||Two more for timing! OK, I'll check but it's a distributorLess ignition so I guess I'll go with the good old stick in the spark plug hole trick.<BR>Thanks guys and please stay tuned.||||reg||4
Z||000007||Alex||03-20-2001||12:57 PM||||OK, I tried the valve timing thing. All that seem fine i.e. #1TDC both valves closed. No indication of a valve gear problem while hand cranking. <BR>I did notice that, after sitting for a week, the plugs were still wet with gas so I've left them out and will reinstall them tonight. See what happens.<BR>What about the TPS or MAP sensors. Think I ought to replace them??<BR>I'm running out of ideas here except fo low compression (and that couldn't happen over night).<BR>IDEAS???||||reg||8
Z||000008||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||01:46 PM||||hrm....any chance you have rugrats running around that could have "fixed" something on the car? The only other option I can suggest since this problem doesn't seem to be getting any better, and I'm not sure how you would be able to verify the valve timing any other way is to remove the timing cover and make sure the gears are indexed properly. After that I would, with the key on, check and see if the injector is possibly sticking open and spraying fuel even when the engine isn't cranking. Also wonder if there is a leaky gasket in the TBI causing fuel to flow freely into the intake.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex||03-20-2001||04:50 PM||||No RugRats around anymore, 862M4inCA. Whatever this is, the car has done it to itself (and me). <BR>I'm on the same track as you are. Has to be too much fuel getting in there some how. Gonna take another run at it tonight. I'll let you know.<BR>Thanks for your reply.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Turbo1dr||03-20-2001||06:48 PM||||All of the valves are moving up and down while someone cranks the engine...correct? <P>If you think it's getting too much fuel remove the fuel soaked spark plugs and replace them with new plugs. Yes I know they are new but sometimes cleaning them won't work (sometimes). Before you put new plugs in squirt some engine oil in all the cylinders, remove the injector harness and crank the engine (plugs still out). This will get the rings sealed better and give you a little more compression on the low compression ones.  After that, put the plugs back in, put the injector harness back on and crank the engine. Be sure to have the throttle near wide open while doing this. If it acts like it wants to start you'll have to use your best judgement on how far the throttle needs to be opened and closed while cranking it.  You may have to remove the plugs to check if they have been coated with the oil you put in.  If they are oiled down clean them off and retry.  If you get too much oil in the cylinders you'll have to keep cleaning the plugs until it's all out. I've done this many times with success with many different types of engines.<P>The coolant temperature or the air temperature sensor may also be reading -40 degrees.  This will make the computer "think" the engine is -40 and will flood it like crazy! The engine will have to be running before the computer sets a code. Some trouble codes are set after a certain amount of time has passed by.<P>(This procedure applies to an engine that has spark, fuel and is correctly in time.)<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000011||btoth||03-20-2001||07:27 PM||||Do you think your gasket may be shot?  Were there any signs of an oil or coolent leak?  My car was running just fine since i bought it and one day after I got out of work I went to start it up and it wouldn't.  Replaced everythig and it turned out my head gaskets (this is my V6) were very bad and my engine was full of coolent and oil where it wasn't supposed to be.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 03-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex||03-20-2001||10:04 PM||||Turbo1dr. I confess that I wasn't looking at all the rockers while hand cranking nor did I have a helper but I'll do that and let you know. Are you implying that I might have a broken cam shaft?<P>Reinstalled the plugs and gave it a whirl. NOTHING!!! Double checked for spark and that was fine. Gas present but not that much this time. ARRGHH!!!<P>BTOTH  That is a posability given the fact that I do have two low compression cylinders. I don't remember if there side by side though. I'll check.<P>Thanks for your replys. Perhaps together we can get this thing going again. I sure do miss her.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Indiana_resto_guy||03-20-2001||11:05 PM||||If you have compression on ALL cylinders, forget about the crankshaft being broken. If it were, you would not get compression out of all of them.<BR>When you crank the engine with the injector unpluged, are you getting fuel spray? If you are install a new injector.<BR>Have you tried a different crank sensor?<BR>Overnight compression loss is very normal! Your lucky if you can get a new engine to hold compression for a few hours.<BR>After all this, I would try another computer. As I recall my '88 coupe did the exact same thing some years ago.<P>Also, first ? should have been:<BR>'Are you getting the check engine light with key on?'<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 03-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Turbo1dr||03-20-2001||11:46 PM||||Just another suggestion.<P>Check for broken valve springs or valves that are starting to sieze in the guides. If these are on the intake side it can cause the engine to backfire in the intake. Your crank sensor is good since you are getting spark and fuel. <P>Are the plugs fuel fouled when you remove them or are they dry looking?  If they are dry pour some gas in the throttle body and then crank the engine. If they are wet it's getting too much fuel. If you cranked the car very long while too much fuel is injected it could be the gas has washed the oil off your rings in the two low cylinders causing the lower compression. Have you put some oil in those cylinders to help bring up the compression?<P>We need a little more info to help you out.<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex||03-21-2001||12:33 AM||||Indiana_resto_guy<BR>Good Point! Although it was the cam shaft I was thinking about, the same applies. If broken, I wouldn't get compression on all cylinders.<BR>There is no fuel comimng out of the injector when unpluged although I didn't try cranking the engine. I did cycle the fuel pump a couple of times though.<BR>I'll see if I can dig up a loaner ECM and try that. No "Check Engine" light.<BR>No codes either.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Alex||03-21-2001||12:46 AM||||Turbo1dr<BR>I haven't checked the valve springs although I do get considerable backfires while cranking with the throttle wide open. Since I've had other cars that will do that, I thought it was normal.<BR>The plugs do come out wet. And it's had more thn enough fuel in the cylinders to wash down the pistons. Good point! I'll shoot som oil in there.<BR>It also seems possible that I have a head gasket problem although I haven't seen any evidence of fluids where they shloudn't be. I suppose it's possable to simply have a leak from one cylinder to another with out compramising a fluid path. <BR>The old guy I bought the car from said that the head had been shaved???!!! I have found some micky mouse things done to the car so it wouldn't supprise me to find that there is a problem in or under the head. Guess I'd better pull it but I'm going to give it one last chance with some oil and see waht happens.<BR>Thanks for your input guys. It's great NOT to be alone in this.<BR>Alex<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-21-2001||02:04 AM||||Shaving the head = decking or planing the head.  This increases compression.  <P>However, 70 psi of compression is enough for the engine to idle.  So I would consider a new TBI (with injector) from this site:<BR> <A HREF="http://geocities.com/fierowarehouse/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://geocities.com/fierowarehouse/engine.html</A> <P>I have a good TBI to sell if you need it, but the price would be a lot more as it is in known working condition and the TPS sensor is brand new.  I guess $50 + shipping.  You should go to the site, they only want about $30.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Alex||03-21-2001||10:28 AM||||LuckyTheyWereEverMade<BR>It does seem like it's getting too much gas but only after cranking it for a while. I'm beginning to believe that it could be a head gasket since I have two "low" compression cylinders and they are together (#2 and #3).<P>Anybody want to comment on the fact that I get backfires through the manifold/throttle body with the throttle wide open? Would this indicate a partially open intake valve?<P>Still huntin' but an engine swap is starting to look good to me!!!||||reg||1
Z||000019||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||10:56 AM||||it isn't normal for an engine to backfire, however in my experience even with the throttle partially open or even closed it would backfire if there was a valve that was remaining open. However that could partially explain the low compression reading, but I can't imagine a valve getting bent unless there were other problems i.e. timing chain, piece of the engine floating in the cylinder. Just wondering if you notice the excess fuel on the plugs after cranking with the throttle open for a while. Might just be dumping too much fuel at WOT causing it to drown out. Also just another thought, have you tested the coil? It's possible that it's a little weak, and once you get a plug under compression it's harder for the spark to jump the gap. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Alex||03-21-2001||11:15 AM||||862M4inCA<BR>Well you and I are thinking along the same lines. That backfire and the low compression seem to point toward a bad valve or two.<P>The module is new and one of the two coils also. I watched the spark on plugs connected to each of the coils and they appear the same.<P>The plugs are always wet when I remove them even though it doesn't seem that I'm getting too much fuel from the injector (I stuffed a rag in the throtle body to test for fuel leakage with the injector disconnected, then, when that didn't seen to be the problem, I re-connected and cranked it a couple of times and had a look at the rag. Didn't seem to be too much fuel there IMO.<BR>BTW, what is WOT?<BR>Alex<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||btoth||03-21-2001||11:41 AM||||WOT = Wide Open Throttle.||||reg||1
Z||000022||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||12:38 PM||||I was just rereading over all of the posts and noticed a good question. Someone had asked if the check engine light is coming on when the key is in the on position, and you said no. Just to verify, this is only when the key is on, not trying to start the engine. If not I would suspect the ECM. When the light comes on it's a sign that the ECM is getting power and is up. might try pulling codes and see if a bad ECM code comes up. I think it's 54 or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Alex||03-21-2001||01:22 PM||||862M4inCA<BR>I guess what I meant to say was that everything was normal. The EMC seem fine and I did pull codes last night. All I got was 12 which indicates the the engine is not running. DA!!! What a surprise THAT is.<BR>Thanks anyway.<P><BR>btoth<BR>WOT Got it. Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Indiana_resto_guy||03-21-2001||07:01 PM||||Check ALL fuse links that feed the ECM (3 total as I recall).||||reg||1
Z||000025||Turbo1dr||03-21-2001||07:05 PM||||Are you sure you got the spark plug wires back on the right cylinders??  Is the battery cranking the engine fast enough??<P><BR>Popping back through the intake is caused by misrouted plug wires, bent or burned intake valve, intake valve sticking in the guide, broke intake valve spring, cam lobe worn off on the exhaust side, headgasket blown in between 2 cylinders.<P><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Fierowrecker||03-21-2001||07:08 PM||||Hate to ask, but, can someone crank the engine while you look through the oil fill hole?<BR>If the rockers move, then cam is rotating.<BR>If the cam is rotating, and you have weak comression on two ajasent cylinders, I would suspect head gasket.<BR>As you said the head was shaved, that is usually done to cure a "warped" head.<BR>Usually, if a head warps, it pulls threads in the block, which weakens the retention of the head.<BR>oh well, all speculation till you pull it apart...<BR>Only God knows what is going on till you take a look...||||reg||1
Z||000027||Alex||03-21-2001||08:49 PM||||Indiana_resto_guy<BR>Well I assumed since I was getting spark and gas that the EMC was working but I'll check 'em. Thanks for the tip.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Alex||03-21-2001||08:53 PM||||Turbo1dr<BR>Yup, I checked the routing several times. Looks like it's coming down to something in the head.<BR>Thanks for your thoughts.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Alex||03-21-2001||09:05 PM||||Fierowrecker<BR>I did rotate the engine by hand and the rockers are moving.<BR>I aggree that the "heads have been shaved" statment sounds suspicious especially considering the fact that this gentelman didn't seem like the type that would just pull the head for the hell of it. And with compression down on the center two cylinders, looks like I gotta dive in. Thanks for the reinforcement.<P>OFF WITH HER HEAD!!!||||reg||1
Z||000030||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-21-2001||10:26 PM||||My head gasket was failing with compression of 120 in the two center cylinders and 160 in the outer two cylinders.<P>The car ran great.  The only clues were white billowing smoke on statup (first 20 minutes or so).  It would hesitate a bit when accelerating, but it could have been any of the sensors as most of them were bad and later replaced.<P>I put in a new headgasket and the compression is 160psi and similar across all four cylinders.  The car gained a little power, but it was not the huge improvement I expected.  All of this happened in the first month I had the car, so I had no idea what it was supposed to run like.<P>You have compression that is below the 70 psi spec in two of your cylinders.  This is low, but I have heard of cars running with less.  <P>Anyhow, it seems like you should do a head gasket.  While you are in there, have a valve job done and replace those head bolts!  You will get to see the tops of the pistons and the condition of the cylinders.  If you luck out like I did, the pistons and cylinder bores will look shiny and clean.  Iron Duke engines have been known to run past 200,000 miles without a rebuild.<P>It may not seem like it right now, but it's worth the effort and money.  A head gasket job is not all that hard (except for torquing the NEW head bolts, it is HARD WORK).<P>Once you have the car apart and have identified the problem, you will have no trouble finding motivation to get it back together.  That car is going to run sooooo much better! ||||reg||1
Z||000031||Alex||03-21-2001||11:49 PM||||LuckyTheyWereEverMade<BR>Thanks for the encouragement. 160psi? Wow, that would be nice.<BR>Mine also hesitates on acceleration and I have always thought that the exhaust was both too wet and too white on start-up.<BR>I'm going to order a set of gaskets tomorrow.<P>On a more personal note, I want to thank everyone for your support. Because of all of you, I feel no frustration over this problem. Only anticipation for driving my great little sports car again.<BR>Thank you.<BR>Stay tuned.<BR>Alex<P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||starfighter007@msn.com||03-22-2001||06:41 PM||||alex I think I would first change your oil and filter if the motor has been flooded Iam guessing allthat gas is in your oil.second I still have a feeling that you have some kind of timing problem I think when some one suggested that you have a broken camshaft Im pretty sure they ment wore out like the the lobes have been ground down to nothing either way you should pull your valve cover gasket off and and crank your motor to make sure all valves  are openig and closing.also if it were me before I change the oil I would try ashot of starting fluid just to see if would start aand if it did shut it off after maybe 5 seconds or so and change the oil. good luck we will be here for you ||||reg||1
Z||000033||Alex||03-22-2001||09:27 PM||||Thanks for you input, starfighter007@msn.com.<BR>I was intending to do the cranking and the oil in th cylinders thing before pulling the head. I really don't think that I've pushen enough gas through her to contaminate the oil.<BR>I did try the starting fluid thing. All I got was a nice fire in and around the throttle body (backfire). Not easy to beat out with a rag!!<BR>At this point, as you could guess, I don't hold out much hope for anything short of checking out the head and valves.<BR>Thanks for the support.<BR>Alex<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Alex||03-23-2001||08:08 PM||||Well, guys, I tried the Oil in the cylinders thing again. Tested compression first at 75, 75, 70, 100 (these numbers are down from the previous test as a result of all the cranking and gas -- probably).<BR>After oiling got 100, 95, 75, 125. Now, I forgot to disconnect the injector so I figured the thing was flooded again, right?<BR>Wrong! I put the plugs back in and tried it. Remarkably, it tried to start more that any other time I've tried it lately but it wouldn't catch. Also noticed that with, what seemed like every other revolution, it backfired through the intake and throttle body. <BR>Looks like I have an intake valve in cyl 3 that's bent, stuck, burnt, dammaged or something. Or possably it's a valve spring.<BR>In either event I guess it's 'off with er head' this weekend.<BR>Anybody disagree?<BR>Alex||||reg||5
Z||000035||MrPBody||03-23-2001||09:14 PM||||I don't know.  I still buy StuGood's first response.  Uneven compression could have many possible causes, but abrupt failure and backfiring suggest an ignition timing or valve timing problem.  If the ignition seems to be working OK (I don't know DIS.) then I suspect broken timing gears -- a common problem on Dukes with 100k+ miles.  If you haven't already checked valve operation with the valve cover removed, do that before you remove the head.<P>Anyhow, good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Turbo1dr||03-23-2001||11:07 PM||||After the oil in the cylinder test, I'm willing to say that you have weak valve springs and the valves are binding in the guides causing them not to close tightly against the seat. This will cause low compression and the popping you hear coming out of the throttle body. <P>A jumped timing chain/gear engine that still has some compression like yours will not pop out of the intake like you say it does.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000037||fyerstarter||03-23-2001||11:36 PM||||You can kill me for suggestion something so simple but I only say it because it happened to me once. Just rotate all your spark plug wires one turn. I was working offa chiltons manual once and I had them lined up exactly like it illustrated. I was having the same prob as you, turns over, had spark, backfires, wet plugs. After two days I finally just rotated em all 1 to the left and fired right up!  Hey its at least worth a try if you havent already :\||||reg||1
Z||000038||Alex||03-24-2001||12:57 AM||||MrPBody<BR>I agree that it does seem like a timing problem until you stop to realise a couple of things like the fact that you can not adjust ign timing on DIS systems and therefore it's not likly to go off time (at least I've never heard of this happening).<BR>Secondly, as I undestand it, when the timing gear gives out on the Duke, it give out completly. Not just skipping a tooth. Also, if the valve timing were off, I probably couldn't get a compression reading. <BR>Still thinkin I have to open her up.<BR>Do you think I should change my userName to Alex the Decapitator?<BR>Off with er Head||||reg||1
Z||000039||Alex||03-24-2001||01:01 AM||||Vernon (Turbo1dr)<BR>I'm with you. Just has to be something fishy in the valve train and with the valves out of time, I couldn't build compression.<BR>Off with er Head!||||reg||1
Z||000040||Alex||03-24-2001||01:08 AM||||fyerstarter<BR>Well, hell. I've tried everything else. I'll give it a whirl and let you know. I have to say though, that it looks right to me. The DIS system fires two cylinders at a time (one in compression and one in exhaust)with two seperate coils and it looks like that's the way it's wired but I'll try it.<BR>Thanks for your suggestion but I still think it'll be<BR>Off with er Head||||reg||1
